<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Using Verbal Descriptions to Create Graphs of Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01011</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Using Verbal Descriptions to Create Graphs of Functions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>367cbb0f-9079-4c64-8da8-c6af15f622f4</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section class="numbered-exercises" id="sect-00001"><title>Cool Down</title>
      <para id="para-00001">To prepare for a backyard party, a parent uses two identical hoses to fill a small pool that is 15 inches deep and a large pool that is 27 inches deep.</para>
      <para id="para-00002">The height of the water in each pool is a function of time since the water is turned on.</para>
      <para id="para-00003">
          <media alt="A woman is filling a large above-ground pool with a hose, while a smaller inflatable kiddie pool with stars is nearby, also filling with water on a grassy area.">
            <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_01.png"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00004">Here are descriptions of three situations. For each situation, sketch the graphs of the two functions on the same coordinate plane, so that <m:math><m:mi>S</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is the height of the water in the small pool after <m:math><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:math> minutes, and <m:math><m:mi>L</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>t</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is the height of the water in the large pool after <m:math><m:mi>t</m:mi></m:math> minutes.</para>
      <para id="para-00005">In both functions, the height of the water is measured in inches.</para>
      <para id="para-00018"><emphasis>Situation 1:</emphasis> Each hose fills one pool at a constant rate. When the small pool is full, the water for that hose is shut off. The other hose keeps filling the larger pool until it is full.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00006">Sketch your interpretation of the graph of situation 1.</para>
            <para id="para-00007"/>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00008">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00009">
                      <media alt="Line graph showing height in inches vs. time in minutes for large and small pools. The small pool levels off at 15 inches after 6 minutes, while the large pool reaches 27 inches after 16 minutes.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_02.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para id="para-00019"><emphasis>Situation 2:</emphasis> Each hose fills one pool at a constant rate. When the small pool is full, both hoses are shut off.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00010">Sketch your interpretation of the graph of situation 2.</para>
            <para id="para-00011"/>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00012">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00013">
                      <media alt="A graph showing water height over time in two pools. The small pools water rises quickly to 9 inches by 6 minutes, then levels off. The large pool rises to 15 inches by 6 minutes and stays constant. Axes: height in inches, time in minutes.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_03.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <para id="para-00020"><emphasis>Situation 3:</emphasis> Each hose fills one pool at a constant rate. When the small pool is full, both hoses are used to fill the large pool until it is full.</para>
      
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00014">Sketch your interpretation of the graph of situation 3.</para>
            <para id="para-00015"/>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00016">Compare your answer: </para>
            <para id="para-00017">
                      <media alt="Line graph showing water height (in inches) over time (in minutes) for a large pool and a small pool. Both rise quickly at first; the small pool stops at 15 inches, while the large pool rises to 27 inches.">
                  <image mime-type="image/svg+xml" src="../../media/Algebra-1_04_12_10_04.svg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
    </section>
  </content></document>